2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinand_Albert_I,_Duke_of_Brunswick-L%C3%BCneburg
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ferdinand Albert I, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand Albert (German Ferdinand Albrecht; 22 May 1636, Brunswick – 25 April 1687, Bevern), Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, was a relative of the princes of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand Albert was the third son of Augustus the Younger, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg. After the father's death in 1666, the three sons quarreled about the heritage, and Ferdinand Albert received a palace in Bevern, some feudal r
2 CONC ights, and a certain amount of money in exchange for his claims to the government of Wolfenbüttel, which was to be ruled jointly by his elder brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand Albert grew more and more eccentric, and at some point his brothers had to send a military force to restore order at his palace. He collected many works of art, which later became part of the Herzog Anton Ulrich Museum. 
2 CONC He died in 1687; his son, Ferdinand Albert, inherited the Principality decades later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand Albert married Christine, daughter of Frederick, Landgrave of Hesse-Eschwege, in 1667. They had the following children that reached adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophia Eleanora (1674-1711), died childless
2 CONT
2 CONT Augustus Ferdinand (1677-1704), died childless
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand Albert (1680-1735)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand Christian (1682-1706), died childless
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernest Ferdinand (1682-1746)
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Ferdinand (1684-1706), died childless
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT At the House of Welf site
2 CONT
2 CONT Allgemeine Deutsche Bibliographie, vol. 6, p. 679-681
