2 CONT Generaal-majoor in the army. Gouverneur van Bergen op Zoom (1643-1658). Gouverneur van Den Bosch (1658-1660). From 1660 special ambassador in England
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis of Nassau (1602 – 28 February 1665) was a Dutch soldier. He was the illegitimate son of Margaretha van Mechelen and Maurice of Nassau, Prince of Orange. He married Isabella, Countess of Hornes in spring 1630, and had ten chi
2 CONC ldren. They had 3 surviving sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Maurits Lodewijk I, lord of de Lek of LaLecq and Beverweerd
2 CONT
2 CONT Willem Adriaan I, lord of Odijk, Kortgene, Zeist and Driebergen
2 CONT
2 CONT Hendrik, lord of Ouwerkerk and Woudenberg
2 CONT
2 CONT He also had seven daughters of whom the two eldest were
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth van Nassau-Beverweert (28 December 1633-18 January 1718), a notable Dutch beauty, who married March 1665 Henry Bennet, 1st Earl of Arlington and had an only daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady Isabella Bennet (c. 1668–7 February 1723) who married 1 August 1672 as a four-year-old child Henry Fitzroy, 1st Duke of Grafton (28 September 1663 – 9 October 1690 d. at the storming of Cork), the illegitimate son of King Cha
2 CONC rles II of England and his mistress the Duchess of Cleveland. They have many descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emilia Butler, Countess of Ossory, a notable beauty like her sister. She was wife of Thomas Butler, Earl of Ossory, and mother of the the 2nd Duke of Ormonde and many other children.
2 CONT
2 CONT A third daughter Wilhelmina married a Dutch nobleman, and had a daughter Anna Elisabeth van Ruytenbergh who married George Cholmondeley, 3rd Earl of Cholmondeley in 1701.
2 CONT
