2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Charlotte, Princess Palatine
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Charlotte of the Palatinate (German: Elisabeth Charlotte von der Pfalz May 27, 1652 – October 9 /December 8, 1722) was a German princess by birth, wife of Philippe, duc d'Orléans, younger brother of Louis XIV of France. 
2 CONC Proud, blunt, opinionated and prolific, her vast correspondence provides a detailed account of the personalities and activities at the court of Louis XIV, her brother-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Born May 27, 1652 in the castle at Heidelberg, to Charles I Louis, Elector Palatine of the Simmern branch of the House of Wittelsbach, and Charlotte of Hesse-Kassel. In childhood she became known as Liselotte - a mixture of her na
2 CONC mes. Her parents were in an unhappy dynastic marriage and in 1653 her father began an affair with Marie Luise von Degenfeld, one of his wife's attendants. He purported to marry her without benefit of a judicial divorce, eventually
2 CONC  prompting Liselotte's mother to return to her paternal home. Their daughter was five years old when she was sent to live with her father's sister, Sophia, wife of Ernest Augustus, Elector of Hanover.
2 CONT
2 CONT She always remembered her time with her aunt as the happiest of her life, although she became close enough to her younger half-sisters to correspond with them at at least weekly after she married. In 1663 Liselotte had to move bac
2 CONC k to Heidelberg where she lived with her stepmother, fifteen half-siblings, and brother, the future Charles II, Elector Palatine.
2 CONT
2 CONT . Several marriage alliances with German princes were considered, but all were declined prior to her engagement to the French king's widowed brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Names
2 CONT
2 CONT She is known by different names and styles in different languages with:
2 CONT
2 CONT Variations of her given names, such as Charlotte Elisabeth, Elisabeth Charlotte and Liselotte
2 CONT
2 CONT Variations of her titles and territorial designations, such as Electoral Princess, Princess Palatine, of the Palatinate, of the Rhine, etc (also in respective forms in French and German)
2 CONT
2 CONT At any rate, the dynastic titles she was entitled to were Countess Palatine of the Rhine and Duchess of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 16, 1671 she was married by proxy at Metz. By prearrangement, after leaving her father's realm but prior to arriving in France, she formally converted to Roman Catholicism. She never saw her homeland again. At the Fren
2 CONC ch court, her husband was known by the traditional honorific of Monsieur. As his wife, Elizabeth Charlotte assumed the style of Madame.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Monsieur
2 CONT
2 CONT The homosexual proclivities of her husband, Monsieur, were well known at court. Elisabeth Charlotte even confided that he needed "rosaries and holy medals draped in the appropriate places to perform the necessary act" with her.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Apparently she did not mind that her husband was homosexual, but objected to money spent on his favorites and the exercise of their influence with him to enrich themselves.[2] She said on the subject:
2 CONT
2 CONT I could put up with it if Monsieur only squandered his money in gaming, but sometimes he gives away as much as 100,000 francs at one swoop, and all the economies fall upon me and the children. That is not at all pleasant, besides 
2 CONC putting me in a position where, as God is my witness, we would have to live entirely on the King's charity, which is a miserable thing.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Madame had apartments at Versailles, use of various châteaux around France, and use of the beautiful château de Saint-Cloud on the outskirts of Paris, which was the couple's main residence when not at the palace of Versailles.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage at first proved to be happy, with the birth of two male heirs. After the death of the couple's first son, the duc de Valois, she experienced depression and worried about her third pregnancy (with Élisabeth Charlotte o
2 CONC f Orléans). After this birth, the relationship between husband and wife was never as close as it had been. The couple had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre Louis d'Orléans, styled duc de Valois (b. St.Cloud, 2 June 1673 – d. Paris, 16 March 1676).
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe, duc II d'Orléans (b. St.Cloud, 2 August 1674 – d. Versailles, 2 December 1723)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Françoise-Marie de Bourbon, the youngest legitimised daughter of Louis XIV and Madame de Montespan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regent of France for Louis XV on the death of his uncle Louis XIV in 1715 and served in that capacity until his own death in 1723.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestor of the House of Orléans, including the modern Orleanist pretender, Henri, comte de Paris, duc de France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Élisabeth Charlotte d'Orléans (b. St.Cloud, 13 September 1676 – d. Commercy, 24 December 1744)
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Leopold, Duke of Lorraine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through Élisabeth Charlotte, she became grandmother of the Holy Roman Emperor Francis I, husband of Empress Maria Theresa of Austria and father of Marie Antoinette.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the birth of their daughter, known as Mademoiselle de Chartres, the couple mutually agreed to cease conjugal relations.[4] Monsieur turned to his mignons, and Madame to her writing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her letters to her aunt Sophia and others created not only a vivid picture of life during the reign of Louis XIV, but also of the Regency era of her son, Philippe. They reflect her alienation from her husband and other family memb
2 CONC ers, as well as her warm relations with the king, and with her son and daughter.
2 CONT
