2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Catherine the Great of Russia - aka Sophia of Anhalt-Zerbst, or Catherine Alekseevna, Empress Catherine II of Russia was the wife of Peter III of Russia. Became estranged from Peter soon after their marriage. She helped to have hi
2 CONC m deposed with the aid of Grigori Orlov, Potemkin, Princess Dashkova, and others.  She succeeded him to the throne on 28 June 1762, and the following day had  Peter III imprisoned.  She proclaimed that Russia was in dire danger fr
2 CONC om foreign domination of her government, the church, and the culture of the country were at stake, and the safety of the nations welfare was in jeopardy.  Her take-over was extraordinary without any bloodshed.  She broke off relat
2 CONC ions with Frederick II of Prussian, and had her friend, Stanislaus Poniatowski, elected King of Poland.  She won two wars with Turkey; extending Russia's border to the Black Sea.  She imported German farmers from her country to po
2 CONC pulate and farm this new aquisition - promising them they would be free of army service.  Her coronation at the Kremlin 22 September 1762 was the most spectacular ever recorded in Russia.  During her reign serfdom and misery among
2 CONC  peasants increased. But the borders of Russia expanded and increased by her large conquests.  She participated in the partition of Poland (1772-1793-95). She won victoris over the Turks in 1768=1772; and by Treaty of Kuchuk Kaina
2 CONC rja (1774) annexed the Crimea.  She thoroughly identified herself with the Russia people - although she herself was German.   She died of natural causes 5 November 1796.
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Original name: Sophie Fredericke Auguste von Anhalt-Zerbst, the daughter of a minor German Prince. She continued the process of Westernization begun by Peter I. Her marriage was unhappy, but she built up a circle of supporters. 10
2 CONC  men occupied roles of lovers and favorites during her reign. Two were Grigory Orlov and Grigory Potenkin. Though German, she completely identified herself with Russian People. She greatly expanded the empire with 2 wars against t
2 CONC he Ottoman empire, and the annexation of the Crimea in 1783.
2 CONT
