1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 312. Heinrich KESSELBURG was born in BACHERSDORF, NEAR BRUGES, GERMANY. His Will is dated 16 AUG. 1729, and Probated, 4 SEPT. 1729. His name was variously spelled Hendrick Kasselberg, Henry Kessleberry, Henry Casse
2 CONC lberry, Henry Casstkeberry. He came to Germantown Pa., probably on the Concord, landing there Oct. 5, 1683 and is first mentioned of record Mar. 7, 1691, when he made an attempt at naturalization, which act was required before he 
2 CONC could own land; he was one of 62 immigrants, including Francis Daniel Pastorius and William Rittenhouse, who sought their citizenship thru an act signed by Thomas Lloyd, Deputy Governor of Pennsylvania. There arose a dispute conce
2 CONC rning the legality of the procedure in such attempted naturalization; whereupon, he and 25 others survived to be re-naturalized, according to Penn. Statutes at large, (Vol. II, p 299), passed 29 Deptember 1709, and confirmed by Qu
2 CONC een in Council, 20 Feb. 1713-14. In 1692, he signed his name "Heinrich Kasselberg, as witness to the marriage of Henry Ffrey and Anna Lovering by Acting Justice of the Peace, Francis Daniel Pastorius, at Germantown. His wife Cathe
2 CONC rine is mentioned as a member of the first Mennonite Church, 23 May 1708. The Mennonites were forerunners of the Baptist Church. Being a very religious people, they felt it unnecessary to mark their graves, feeling that God would 
2 CONC know where to find them, which was all that mattered.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1683 William Penn invited the Mennonites to settle in Pennsylvania. Soon many of them from the lower Rhine, around Crefeld, and the Netherlands, settled in and about Germantown.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mennonites are likely descended from the Waldenses, who were numerous in Holland and Flanders. They probably date from the 12th century opposition to Roman Catholicism. Their enemies, who presecuted them severely, nevertheless
2 CONC  bore testimony of the purity of their lives, their thrift, frugality, and homely virtues. They were husbandmen and artisans, and many were weavers. The Mennonites and Quakers were very close, after being driven up and down the Rh
2 CONC ine for 150 years, they were ready to come to America, a wild country.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~markie/d129.htm#P1503
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich Kesselberg was baptized on 1 Oct. 1661 in the Evangelical Reformed Church at Mulheim on the Ruhr in Baakendorf, County of Broich (Burgee), Germany.   His father was Heinrich Kesselberg and his mother was unknown Keinburch
2 CONC .  Heinrich Kesselberg arrived in Pennsylvania on board the ship Concord  in 1683, at the age of twenty-two.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He was naturalized on 7 Mar 1691 and again, for some technical reason, in 1708. His Anglicized  name  became Henry Castleberry.  He purchased 89 acres of land on 22 Jun 1709 from Edward Farmer in Farmerstown, PA, which is in the v
2 CONC icinity of the present Whitemarsh Post Office in Whitemarsh Township, Montgomery County; about eight miles northwest of Germantown, PA.  He married Catherine Levering sometime between 1695 and 1698. Henry died in March 1729 and Ca
2 CONC therine died in 1767 or 1768, both in Germantown, PA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Henry and Catherine Castleberry were:
2 CONT
2 CONT *   2i. Derrick Castleberry:  b. unknown - d 1765
2 CONT *   2ii. Paul Castleberry:  b. unknown - d Apr 1748 (His descendants founded Castleberry, AL,  in 1830)
2 CONT *   2iii. John Castleberry: unknown
2 CONT *   2iv. William Castleberry: (married Margaret Davis; probable direct ancestor of James Castleberry.
2 CONT *   2v. Eve Castleberry:  (married John Bassett 20 Feb. 1723 in Christ Church, Philadelphia, PA)
2 CONT *   2vi. Mary Castleberry:  (married Philip Schrack)
2 CONT *   2vii. Katherine Castleberry: (married Hugh Hamilton)
2 CONT *   2viii. Elizabeth Castleberry: (married unknown Davis)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.marciesalaskaweb.com/rogerslineage4.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT About KESSELBERG, HEINRICH
2 CONT
2 CONT Please notice a name change from Kesselberg to Castleberry for his children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naturalized in 1691, settled in Germantown, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry came to America , probably on the Concord, landing there Oct 5 1683; first mentioned on a record on 7 Mar 1691, when he made an attempt of naturalization. He had to be naturalized before he could own land. One of 62 immigran
2 CONC ts who tried for naturalization.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1692, he signed his name HEINRICH KASSELBERG, as a witness to a marriage of Henry Ffrey and Anna Lovering at Germantown, PA. His wife Catherine was mentioned as a member of the first mennonite Church, 23 May 1708. The Mennonite
2 CONC s were forerunners of the Baptist Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT `A very religious people, they felt it unnecessary to mark their graves, feeling that God would know where to find them, which was all that mattered.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1683 William Penn invited the Mennonites to settle in Pennsylvania. Soon many of them from the lower Rhine, around Crefeld, and the Netherlands, settled in and about Germantown.
2 CONT
2 CONT `Perkoiomen Region` Vol. 12, 1934; in Penn State Library: Will if Henry Casselbery, made August 16, 1729, proved 4 September 1729. Stated: `Katherine, my wife, and son Dirick to be my Executors. My son Dirich to have half of all. 
2 CONC My son John to have half of all. Son Paul Casstelberry to have X 10. Son WiIliam Casstelberry X 8 .Daughter Eva Bassett X* Daughter Elizabeth Casstelberry X 8.(Married Davis). Daughter Mary Casstelberry X 8. (Married Phillip Shrac
2 CONC k). Daughter Catherine Casstleberry X 8. Witnesses: John Casper; Thomas Lock; Richard Jones.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed Henry (His X mark) Casselberry.
2 CONT
