1 NOTE Soon after Charles succeeded to the throne, Sweden, with extensive 
2 CONT possessions on the Baltic, was threatened by a coalition of Frederick IV, 
2 CONT king of Denmark, Augustus II, king of Poland (1670-1733), and Peter I, 
2 CONT czar of Russia, which resulted in the Great Northern War (1700-21). In 
2 CONT 1700, Charles invaded Denmark and quickly forced Frederick to sign the 
2 CONT Peace of Travendal (now Traventhal). Charles hastened to the Baltic and 
2 CONT rapidly brought his army of 8000 men to the Swedish stronghold, Narva, 
2 CONT Estonia, which was beleaguered by 40,000 Russians. The disciplined Swedish 
2 CONT troops, although wearied by forced marches, totally routed the Russians in 
2 CONT November 1700. Charles then turned to conquer Poland, which was overrun by 
2 CONT the Swedish troops. Augustus was driven into Saxony, and Charles obtained 
2 CONT the election of his ally Stanislas I Leszczyñski as king of Poland in 
2 CONT 1705. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles then marched into Saxony, and Augustus, by the Treaty of 
2 CONT Altranstädt of 1706, was forced to recognize Stanislas. Charles was now at 
2 CONT the height of his power; with a disciplined army holding Germany in awe, 
2 CONT he spurned peace overtures from Peter. Determined to humble Russia, he 
2 CONT began an invasion of the country in September 1707. He penetrated into the 
2 CONT interior of Russia, his army harassed along the way, suffered through two 
2 CONT severe winters and turned south. On July 8, 1709, while besieging Poltava, 
2 CONT Ukraine, he was attacked by the Russian army. Within three days, all his 
2 CONT previous military success was undone in one disastrous engagement. Charles 
2 CONT barely escaped into Turkish territory. He induced Sultan Ahmed III 
2 CONT (1673-1736) to attack Russia. In 1711 Peter was able to escape from a 
2 CONT precarious position on the Prut River. The Swedish monarch spent the next 
2 CONT three years in intrigues to induce Turkey to attack Russia again. When he 
2 CONT found that his plots were of no avail, he defied the Turkish power and was 
2 CONT imprisoned. He escaped in 1714 and reached Stralsund, a Swedish possession 
2 CONT in Pomerania. The city was besieged by a combined force of Danes, 
2 CONT Prussians, and Saxons for a year before it surrendered. Charles again 
2 CONT escaped, reached Sweden, and raised another army. He began an invasion of 
2 CONT Norway in 1717. During this struggle he was killed at Frederikshald on 
2 CONT November 30, 1718. He was succeeded on the throne by his sister Ulrika 
2 CONT Eleonora, who began the process of negotiating peace to end the war that 
2 CONT had cost Sweden its rank as a great power in the Baltic region.
