1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Christian IV, Count Palatine of Zweibrücken-Birkenfeld''' (6 September 1722 in Bischweiler – 5 November 1775 in Herschweiler-Pettersheim) was Duke of Zweibrücken from 1735 to 1775.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian IV was born in Bischweiler on 6 September 1722, the son of Christian III, Count Palatine of Zweibrücken and Caroline of Nassau-Saarbrücken.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1751 he married, morganatically, Maria Johanna Camasse (1734–1807, created Countess Forbach in 1757). They had six children, who were unable to succeed to their father's Duchy due to the morganatic nature of their parents' marr
2 CONC iage at first, but in 1792 were allowed to carry the name Freiherr von Zweibrücken:
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian, Count of Forbach, Marquis of Deux-Ponts (1752–1817), Royal Bavarian General der Infanterie
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe Guillaume (later renamed to Wilhelm), Count of Forbach, Viscount of Deux-Ponts (1754–1807)
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Anna Caroline von Zweibrücken (1756–1806)
2 CONT
2 CONT Karl Ludwig, Baron of Zweybrücken (1759–63)
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth Auguste Friederike von Zweibrücken (1766–1836)
2 CONT
2 CONT Julius August Maximilian, Baron of Zweybrücken (1771–73)
2 CONT
2 CONT His grandson Christian Freiherr von Zweibrücken (1782–1859, son of Philipp Guillaume) was a Royal Bavarian General der Kavallerie and Generalkapitän of the Hartschiere.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_IV,_Count_Palatine_of_Zweibr%C3%BCcken
2 CONT
2 CONT En français: Christian IV de Deux-Ponts-Birkenfeld https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christian_IV_de_Deux-Ponts-Birkenfeld
