1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landgravine_Christine_of_Hesse-Rotenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Landgravine Christine of Hesse-Rotenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Christine
2 CONT
2 CONT Princess of Carignan
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Louis Victor, Prince of Carignan
2 CONT
2 CONT Detail
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Victor Amadeo, Prince of Carignan
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopoldina, Princess of Melfi
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Louise, Princess of Lamballe
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriella, Princess of Lobkowicz
2 CONT
2 CONT Caterina, Princess of Paliano
2 CONT
2 CONT Eugenio, Count of Villafranca
2 CONT
2 CONT Full name
2 CONT
2 CONT English: Christine of Hesse-Rotenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Italian: Cristina d'Assia-Rotenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT German: Christine von Hessen-Rotenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Ernest Leopold, Landgrave of Hesse-Rotenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Eleonora Maria of Löwenstein-Wertheim-Rochefort[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	21 November 1717(1717-11-21)
2 CONT
2 CONT Rotenburg an der Fulda in Hesse, Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	1 September 1778 (aged 60)
2 CONT
2 CONT Palazzo Carignano, Turin, Italy
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	Basilica of Superga, Italy
2 CONT
2 CONT Christine of Hesse-Rotenburg (Christine Henriette; 21 November 1717 – 1 September 1778) was a Landgravine of the German state of Hessen-Rheinfels-Rotenburg. She was the Princess of Carignan by marriage and mother of the princesse 
2 CONC de Lamballe as well as the Prince of Carignan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Titles, styles, honours and arms
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 3.1 Titles and styles
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 References and notes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 6 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Christine Henriette was born in Rotenburg the youngest child of the Landgrave of Hesse-Rotenburg and his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was one of 10 children[1]; her older sister Polyxena Christina of Hesse-Rotenburg was married in 1730 to the future Charles Emmanuel III of Sardinia and had issue; Polyxena Christina has present descendants that include the Cl
2 CONC aimant Duke of Parma; the Pretending King of the Two Sicilies and the reigning Grand Duke of Luxembourg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another sister, Landgravine Caroline was the wife of the French Prime Minister, Louis Henri de Bourbon and was the princesse de Condé till her early death in 1741.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the marriage of her oldest sister Polyxena Christina, Christine was engaged to the Prince of Carignan. Her future husband was Prince Louis Victor of Savoy a member of the so called House of Savoy-Carignan. Louis Victor was t
2 CONC he eldest surviving child of Victor Amadeus, Prince of Carignan and his wife Maria Vittoria Francesca of Savoy[2]. Louis Victor was a male line descendant of Thomas Francis, Prince of Carignan who founded the line with his wife Ma
2 CONC rie de Bourbon. The Carignan family were a cadet branch of the House of Savoy, Dukes of Savoy from 1416 – 1718 and then Kings of Sardinia from 1720 till 1861.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christine was married to Louis Victor on 4 May, 1740 at the age of 22. The next year Christine would become the Princess of Carignan by right of her husband who succeeded to the Principality. The seigneury of Carignan had belonged
2 CONC  to the Savoys since 1418, and the fact that it was part of Piedmont, only twenty km. south of Turin, meant that it could be a "princedom" for Thomas in name only, being endowed neither with independence nor revenues of substance.
2 CONC [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In Savoy, Christine was known as Cristina [Enrichetta]. In 1742, Christine gave birth to her first child who was named Charlotte Maria Louise, Princess of Savoy. Charlotte would die unmarried. Her second child, born at the Palazzo
2 CONC  Carignano was named Victor Amadeus and was the great grand father of the future Victor Emmanuel II of Italy. Her second daughter, Leopoldina Maria, was later the Princess of Melfi as wife of Giovanni Andrea Doria-Pamphilj-Landi. 
2 CONC Her next daughter Polyxena Teresa died unmarried aged 16 in 1762. Princess Gabrielle married the Prince of Lobkowicz in 1769. Her fifth daughter was her most famous; Princess Maria Teresa Louisa di Savoia, better known as the prin
2 CONC cesse de Lamballe and best friend of Marie Antoinette.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Teresa married in 1767[1] to the prince de Lamballe, heir to one of the largest fortunes of the era and great grandson of Louis XIV and Madame de Montespan. Widowed the next year, Christines daughter was given a large fortun
2 CONC e making her an extremely wealthy women in her own right.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1751 Christine had another son who died in 1753. Another son Louis Victor of Savoy, Count of Villafraca founded the Villafranca line which survive to this day. Her youngest daughter Caterina married into the Colonna family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christine died at the Palazzo Carignano in Turin on the night of August 31 - 1 September, 1778, and was followed less than three months later by her husband. Since 1835 her grave has been in Turin's Basilica of Superga, as is that
2 CONC  of her husband. At her death the Gazette de France wrpte a small epitaph for her in honour of her daughter Madame de Lamballe:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Tuesday, the 31st of last month[4] , princesse Christine Henriette de Hesse Rheinfels, wife of Louis Victor Amédée de Savoie, Prince de Carignan, died in this city [Turin], after a lingering and painful illness. She was born t
2 CONC he 24th November, 1717[4].
2 CONT
2 CONT Her present descendants include the pretending cousins Vittorio Emanuele, Prince of Naples[1] and Amedeo, 5th Duke of Aosta. There is also Prince Lorenz of Belgium, Archduke of Austria-Este.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Charlotte Maria Luisa of Savoy (Turin, 17 August 1742 - 20 February, 1794) died unmarried; was a nun;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prince Vittorio Amadeo of Savoy (Turin, 31 October 1743 - September 1780) married Joséphine de Lorraine and had issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Leopoldina Maria of Savoy (Turin, 21 December 1744 – Rome, 17 April 1807) married Prince Andrea IV Doria Pamphili Landi, 8th Prince of Melfi and had issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Polyxena Teresa of Savoy (Turin, 31 October 1746 - 20 December 1762) died unmarried;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Gabrielle of Savoy (Turin, 17 May, 1748 - Vienna, 10 April, 1828) married Ferdinand Philipp Josef, Prince of Lobkowicz and had issuel currant House of Lobkowicz descend from her;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Maria Teresa Luisa of Savoy (Turin, 8 September 1749 – Paris, 3 September 1792) married Louis Alexandre Joseph de Bourbon, prince de Lamballe; murdered in the French Revolution;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prince Tommaso of Savoy (Turin, 6 March, 1751 - 23 July, 1753)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Prince Eugenio of Savoy (Turin, 21 October 1753 - 30 June 1785) married Elisabeth Anne Magon Boisgarin and had issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Princess Caterina Maria Luisa Francesca of Savoy (Turin, 4 April 1762 - 4 September 1823) married Don Filippo Giuseppe Francesco Colonna, 9th Prince of Paliano, descendant of Marie Mancini[1]; had issue from which the presen
2 CONC t Princes of Paliano descend[1];
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Titles, styles, honours and arms
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Titles and styles
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 21 November 1717 – 4 May 1740 Her Serene Highness Landgravine Christine of Hesse-Rotenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 May 1740 – 4 April 1741 Her Highness Princess Louis Victor of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 April 1741 – 1 September 1778 Her Highness] the Princess of Carignan
2 CONT
