2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Despite being elected by ever more of the people as parliamentary reform progressed since 1832, the interrelated elected leaders are interrelated with monarchy too, with the older system. Thus
2 CONT Hugh Capet  b941, King of France 987-96
2 CONT Robert II Capet  b972, King of France 996-1031
2 CONT Malcolm II mac Cinaeda, King of Scotland 1005-34
2 CONT Henri I Capet  b1008, King of France 1031-60 and co-regent from 1027
2 CONT William I of Normandy  b1028, Duke of Normandy, King of England 1066-87
2 CONT Malcolm III Canmore  b1033, King of Scotland 1058-93 - with St Margaret Atheling  b1046, Queen 1070-93
2 CONT David I Canmore  b1084, King of Scotland 1124-53
2 CONT Henry I Beauclerc  b1068, King of England 1100-35
2 CONT Henry II d'Anjou  b1133, King of England 1154-89
2 CONT William I Canmore  b1143, King of Scotland 1165-1214, with English overlordship 1174-89
2 CONT Roger Mortimer  b1287, Regent of England 1327-30
2 CONT Edward I Plantagenet,  b1239, King of England 1272-1307
2 CONT Robert I Bruce  b1274, King of Scotland 1306-29
2 CONT Edward II Plantagenet  b1284, King of England 1307-27
2 CONT Edward III Plantagenet  b1312, King of England 1327-77 - with his son John of Gaunt  b1340, who fathered 20 ch, after his dad had fathered 14
2 CONT Robert II Stewart  b1316, King of Scotland 1371-90
2 CONT Henry IV Plantagenet  b1366, King of England 1399-1413, one of the 20
2 CONT Charles VI de Valois  b1368, King of France 1380-1422
2 CONT Henry V Plantagenet  b1387, King of England  1413-22
2 CONT Edward IV Plantagenet   b1442, King of England 1461-70 + 1471-83 - with Elizabeth Wydevill  b1437, 
2 CONT Queen from 1464 - and Edward's stepfather Richard of York  b1411, Regent of England 1453-5
2 CONT Henry VII Tudor  b1457, King of England 1485-1509
2 CONT Henry VIII Tudor  b1491, King of England 1509-47
2 CONT Mary Rose Tudor  b1496, Queen of France 1514-5
2 CONT Charles I Stuart  b1600, King of Britain 1625-49
2 CONT Charles II Stuart  b1630, King of Scotland 1649-51, Britain 1660-85
2 CONT James VII and II Stuart  b1633, VII of Scotland II of England, King of Britain 1685-8
2 CONT George II Hanover  b1683, King of Britain 1727-60 - and his son William Duke of Cumberland  b1721, 
2 CONT winning government army leader against the Jacobites 1746
2 CONT George III Hanover  b1738, King of Britain 1760-1820
2 CONT Napoleon I Buonaparte  b1769, self-proclaimed Emperor of France 1804-14
2 CONT George IV Hanover  b1762, Prince Regent 1811-20, King of Britain 1820-30
2 CONT William IV Hanover  b1765, King of Britain 1830-7
2 CONT George V Saxe-Coburg-Gotha  b1865, King of Britain 1910-36
2 CONT Albert Windsor (George VI)  b1896, King of Britain 1936-52
2 CONT Elizabeth Bowes-Lyon  b1900, the Queen Mother
2 CONT Elizabeth II Windsor  b1952, the Queen
2 CONT Diana Spencer  b1961
2 CONT See the continuous presence of the Hanoverians 1727-1837, kings interrelated with PMs for the first century of the office's existence.
2 CONT At least Buonaparte, original Corsican spelling includes the U, is balanced by Horatio Nelson b1758 and PM Arthur Wellesley b1769, but Wellington was not a man of the people, was the last PM to be a Duke and be against the ninetee
2 CONC nth century great reforms of parliament.
2 CONT It is right to refer to Britain for the Stuarts after the Union of Crowns. As they ruled with only intermittent parliaments, they were the real ruler so they had united both countries under 1 ruler. So Scotland fully ceased to be 
2 CONC independent in 1603, but became it again for the period 1688-1707 when continuous parliaments began as a new standard following the Dutch invasion that ousted the Stuarts, and it had a parliamentary administration.
