1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Father: Jacob * ULRICH c: 20 Jul 1656 in Freiburg, , Baden-Württemberg , GE Mother: Anna * FREY [Ulrich] b: 1660 in , , , Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Elizabeth WAGONER [Ulrich] b: ABT 1686 in , Kastelland, Reinland, Germany c: 22 Jan 1696 in Katholisch, Buch, Kastelland, Reinland, Germ. Married: ABT 1705 in <Swebeland, Mannheim, Baden, GER (1696 birth does not make s
2 CONC ense. Typo, and the date should be 22 Jan 1686??)> Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen ULRICH II (Emigrant 1725) b: ABT 1710 in Swabaland, Mannheim, Baden, Germany
2 CONT David ULRICH b: 16 Mar 1716/1717
2 CONT Samuel ULRICH (unm) b: 1716 in , , , Germany
2 CONT George ULRICH (Emigrant-1725) b: ABT 1719 in Swebeland, Mannheim, Baden Germany
2 CONT Elizabeth ULRICH [Greib] (Emigrant-1725) b: 1724 in Swebeland, Mannheim, Baden Germany
2 CONT Susanna ULRICH [Martin] (Emigrant) b: ABT 1727 in Swebeland, Mannheim, Baden Germany
2 CONT Daniel ULRICH Sr. (Emigrant-1725) b: ABT 1718/1726 in Swebeland, Mannheim, Baden Germany
2 CONT Marriage 2 Anna Margretha MAKIN [Ulrich] b: 1709 in Schriesheim, Mannheim, Baden, Germany Married: 10 Jan 1729/1730 Children
2 CONT
2 CONT John ULRICH (Emigrant) b: 1710/1720 in Swebeland, Mannheim, Baden Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT STEPHEN ULRICH “ULERY” SR. was born around 1688 in Mannheim, Baden-Württemberg, Germany. He is thought to be the son of Jacob John Ulrich and Anna Frey. He is also thought to have married Elizabeth (possibly Waggoner). They had 7 
2 CONC children: Stephen (~1710-1789); David; Samuel; George; Elizabeth; Susanna; and Daniel. He likely immigrated with his family around 1725-1727 at the beginning of the Palatine immigration. By 1738 he was naturalized and was living i
2 CONC n northern Maryland (which is now York Co., Pennsylvania) in an area called Conewago. He purchased his land directly from John Digges, who was warranted 10,000 acres, where Conewago was located. Records also note that he was a far
2 CONC mer/planter. The Brethren Congregation of Little Conewago was also created in 1738 by “Eldrick, Dierdorff, Bigler, Gripe, Studsman, and others.” He again purchased land in 1743 originating from a Maryland deed. He also began selli
2 CONC ng off parts land soon after its purchase. This area in southern Pennsylvania was being contested, in a conflict called Cresap’s War, and Pennsylvania was issuing deeds to the same region. The only area not contested was land gran
2 CONC ts from the original Digges’ grant. He is thought to have died in 1749 in Conewago/Hanover, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note
2 CONT Note: He once owned land in what is now Adams Co. PA.
2 CONT On 4-June-1738 the government of Maryland granted a patent of naturalization to Stephen Ulderey (sic), a native of High Germany but now of Baltimore County, planter, and to his children: Stephen, George, Daniel, John, Elizabeth, a
2 CONC nd Susanna. (From Maryland Hall of Records, Annapolis, commission record 1733-1738, page 57.) At the time, Baltimore County extended into what is now the southern part of York County, Pennsylvania. Year of birth is from Brethren E
2 CONC ncyclopedia, page 1285.
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY:Oral History of Ulrick Family;u462;Letter Private Document:Kinsey's My Family Tree;.......Stephen Ulrick was born about 1680 near the German and Swiss border.Some records give His birthplace as Swebeland,Germany....He ha
2 CONC d two brother ,John and another whose name is unknow...all members of the German Baptist Church.Early in the eighteenth century they left their European home to escape religious persecution and came to the New World. They settled 
2 CONC east of the mountains--probably first in northern Maryland and later removing to Huntingdon Co.,Pa. near Hollidaysburg...Now Blair Co.
2 CONT Thease three brother were all short and heavy set.They married three very tall sisters.. Stephen(our ancestor) also had three sons,David, Samuel, and Stephen.They were all German Baptist preachers.
2 CONT _________________________________________________________
2 CONT The Elusive Stephen Ulrich
2 CONT Like so many other Brethren, I am a descendant of Stephen Ulrich, native of Germany, about whom so much has been said and so little is known. One thing that is known is from the Council of Maryland, "Commission Book No. 82," which
2 CONC  contains miscellaneous entries from 1733 to 1773, one of which, from page 57 of the original manuscript, says: "Ulderey, Stephen, Planter of Baltimore county, native of High Germany, naturalized 4 June, 1738; and his children Ste
2 CONC phen, George, Daniel, John, Elizabeth and Susanna."
2 CONT Also listed on pages 57-58 of "Commission Book No. 82" were six of Stephen Ulderey's neighbors, all of whom were described as Planters and natives of High Germany, and all of whom were known to reside at or near Digges Choice on L
2 CONC ittle Conewago Creek. According to Morgan Edwards, writing in 1770, the Little Conewago Congregation of Brethren was started in 1738, by Eldrick, Dierdorff, Bigler, Gripe, Studsman and others under the leadership of Daniel Leather
2 CONC man. It is commonly thought that Eldrick was Ulrich, Gripe was Greib/Cripe, and Studsman was Stutzman. All were Brethren, friends, neighbors, and related by marriage. It is also commonly thought that Eldrick and Ulderey were one a
2 CONC nd the same.
2 CONT As far as I know, the 1738 Maryland document is the earliest source naming the Brethren progenitor Stephen Ulrich and his children. Nothing that has been said about Stephen Ulrich's life before 4 June, 1738, has been documented to
2 CONC  my knowledge. Ulrich researchers do not know where he lived in Germany, whom he married, who bore his children, when he immigrated, etc.
2 CONT I would like to re-open the discussion about this important Brethren progenitor by welcoming others' ideas about Stephen Ulrich's life before 1738, as well as the sources supporting these ideas. Some of the ideas may turn out to b
2 CONC e without grounds, but, who knows.......maybe we will all learn something.
2 CONT -- Dwayne Wrightsman
2 CONT Fact: Arrival (1738) Baltimore, Maryland
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Stephen Ulrich Sr. was the subject of research posted by Roberta Estes in her blog DNAeXplained - Genetic Genealogy titled 'Stephen Ulrich Sr., (born c1690), The Conewago Settlement and the Border War, 52 Ancestors #136', dated Oc
2 CONC tober 23, 2016.
2 CONT "Family Tree," database, FamilySearch (http://familysearch.org : modified 20 February 2023, 15:10), entry for Hans Stephen Ulrich (PID https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/4:1:GFTD-1NC ); contributed by various users.
2 CONT Maryland Hall of Records, Annapolis, commission record 1733-1738, Pg. 57
2 CONT A History of the Church of the Brethren in southern district of Pennsylvania, pg. 190
2 CONT following from My.ohio.voyager.net/wkfisher/Ullery.html There are many spelling variants which evolved from the original German Ulrich. In America there are two known strains of Ulricks, both strains using different spelling varia
2 CONC nts. We are descended from the earlier strain whose immigrant forefathers were: Stephen I born ca 1680; John; and one unnamed (possibly George). Reputedly they were physicall short and stout and married three tall English sisters.
2 CONC  Possibly, they married in the English seaman area of Chincoteaque Island, where, according to one tradiition, they raised ponies (rounded up small wild horses known as Chincoteaque ponies), which they swam to the mainland and sol
2 CONC d. The first known acquisitions of the Ulrichs were in the area of the present Adams Co, Pennsylvania. The earliest records of that area were recorded in Chester Co. The county status of that part of Adams County successively chan
2 CONC ged to Lancaster Co. on 14 Oct 1728; to York Co. on 14 Oct 1748; and to Adams Co. in 1800. Probably it was in the Adams County area that immigrant John Ulrich, while hunting wild turkeys, fell down a precipice and was fatally impa
2 CONC led on a tree snag. It has been written than an Eldrick (probably Stephen Ulrich II) and a Studsman (probably Jacob Stutzman, Sr.) were two of the organizers of the Little Conewago Congregation, formed about 1738 near Hanover Penn
2 CONC sylvania. Therefore, the prevalent migrations of the Ulrichs, and associated German Baptist families, were sequentially: to the Conacocheaque Creek Valley of the present Franklin Co, Pennsylvania, and the present Washington Co. Ma
2 CONC ryland; to the Morrison's Cove area of the originally large Bedford Co., Pennsylvania; to the southwester and western areas of Ohio, especially Montgomery Co.; and to the central and northern areas of Indiana. Stephen Ulrich I (Fr
2 CONC om unpublished information supplied in July 1980 by Fred E. Ullery.
2 CONT
