1 CONT George I of Great Britain From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT George I, George Louis (May 28, 1660 - June 11, 1727) was the first Hanoverian King  of the Kingdom of Great Britain(as well as King of Ireland) from August 1, 1714, to June 11, 1727. George, unlike most British kings in the past 
1 CONC 300 years, did not speak English -- he spoke German and was ridiculed by many of his subjects for this and for his succession of German mistresses, earning him the nickname Geordie Whelps.
1 CONT
1 CONT George I King of Great Britain, Ireland
1 CONT Elector of Hanover George was born on May 28, 1660 in Hanover, Germany, and was the son of the Electress Sophia of Hanover who was a granddaughter of King James I of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1682, he married Sophia, Princess of Zelle, and they had two children.: 1, King George II of Great Britain _II_of_Great_Britain - (November 10, 1683 - October 25 , 1760). 2. Sophia Dorothea, later Queen consort of Prussia - (Ma
1 CONC rch 26, 1687 - June 28, 1757. Mother of King Frederick II of Prussia, known as Frederick the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT George also had at least three illegitimate children by his mistress Ehrengard Melusine von der Schulenburg, whom he created sucessively Duchess of Munster and Duchess of Kendal.
1 CONT
1 CONT George was elector of Hanover from 1698 until his death in 1727.
1 CONT
1 CONT George divorced Sophia in 1694. She had committed adultery with the Swedish count  Philip Christoph von Königsmarck, whose murder at the command of Countess von Platen he seems to have countenanced, but the grounds for the divorce
1 CONC  were not George's or Sophia's adultery, but Sophia's alleged "abandonment" of her husband. George had Sophia imprisoned in the Castle of Ahlden, where she remained until her death in 1726.
1 CONT
1 CONT George's mother died only a few weeks before her cousin, Queen Anne of Great Britain, and thus it was George who inherited the throne on Queen Anne's death on August 1, 1714. The Hanoverian ruling family were the closest Protestan
1 CONC t relatives, but they faced opposition from supporters of the Stuart pretender. An unsuccessful rebellion took place in 1715, in an attempt to put James Francis Edward Stuart, the son of King James II of England on the throne. See
1 CONC  Jacobitism.
1 CONT
1 CONT George never learned to speak English properly, and divided his time between Britain and his other territory of Hanover. He had a poor relationship with his own son, George, who was created Prince of Wales shortly after arriving i
1 CONC n Britain. The birth of a second grandson in 1717 was the occasion for a family quarrel, and the Prince of Wales was banished from the royal residence along with his wife and children. Some reconciliation was eventually achieved, 
1 CONC but they were never on cordial terms.
1 CONT
1 CONT The fact that George spoke poor English and was frequently out of the country (allied with the fact that he was, in the opinion of most contemporaries, not very intelligent), greatly aided the development of parliamentary and cabi
1 CONC net government. In 1721 Sir Robert Walpole became the King's first minister, and he is generally regarded as the first true Prime Minister _of_the_United_Kingdom: a minister who was appointed by the King but who held office becaus
1 CONC e he could command a majority in the House of Commons. The other members of the Cabinet held office at Walpole's pleasure, not the King's. Although later Kings (notably George III _of_the_United_Kingdom) tried to reverse this shif
1 CONC t in power, it proved to be permanent.
1 CONT
1 CONT George I died at Osnabrück in 1727 from a stroke while on his way to Hanover, where he was buried. His son succeeded him as King George II of Great Britain.
