0 @N6000000002188395973@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT      George II (reigned 1727-60), at the age of 60, was the last British sovereign to fight alongside his soldiers, at the Battle of Dettingen in 1743 in Germany, against the French. Like his father, for much of his reign George's
1 CONC  political options were limited by the strength of the Jacobite cause (James Stuart the Old Pretender, and then his son, Charles Edward Stuart), with which many of the Tories were linked. George's reign was threatened in 1745 when
1 CONC  Charles Edward Stuart, the Young Pretender, landed in Scotland. After some initial success (which led to the popularity of the national anthem in its current form becoming popular among the Hanoverian loyalists), Charles was defe
1 CONC ated at the Battle of Culloden in April 1746 and the
1 CONT Jacobite threat was over. The foundations of the industrial revolution were
1 CONT laid during George's reign, with new levels of production in industries such as coal and shipbuilding and also in agriculture, together with a rapid rise in population. Overseas, trade was boosted by successes such as Clive's vict
1 CONC ories in India at Arcot (1751) and Plassey (1757) which placed Madras and Bengal under British control, and Wolfe's capture of French-held Quebec in 1759 (part of a successful campaign which transferred Canada with its wealthy tra
1 CONC de in fish and fur from Bourbon to British rule). As the country prospered and George's reign lengthened, George's early unpopularity (he did not travel far in England, and much preferred Hanover) changed into a general respect. T
1 CONC he king's eldest son, Frederick, died in 1751. George's grandson therefore inherited the throne, on George's death in 1760.
1 CONT He is the last monarch to be buried at Westminster Abbey.
