0 @N6000000005598973510@ NOTE (r. 1727-60)
1 CONT
1 CONT George II, at the age of 60, was the last British sovereign to fight alongside his soldiers, at the Battle of Dettingen in 1743 in Germany, against the French. Like his father, for much of his reign George's political options were
1 CONC  limited by the strength of the Jacobite cause (James Stuart the Old Pretender, and then his son, Charles Edward Stuart), with which many of the Tories were linked. George's reign was threatened in 1745 when Charles Edward Stuart,
1 CONC  the Young Pretender, landed in Scotland. After some initial success (which led to the national anthem in its current form becoming popular among the Hanoverian loyalists), Charles was defeated at the Battle of Culloden in April 1
1 CONC 746 and the Jacobite threat was over.
1 CONT
1 CONT The foundations of the industrial revolution were laid during George's reign, with new levels of production in industries such as coal and shipbuilding and also in agriculture, together with a rapid rise in population. Overseas, t
1 CONC rade was boosted by successes such as Clive's victories in India at Arcot (1751) and Plassey (1757), which placed Madras and Bengal under British control, and Wolfe's capture of French-held Quebec in 1759 (part of a successful cam
1 CONC paign which transferred Canada with its wealthy trade in fish and fur from French to British rule).
1 CONT
1 CONT As the country prospered and George's reign lengthened, his early unpopularity (he did not travel far in England, and much preferred Hanover) changed into a general respect. The King's eldest son, Frederick, died in 1751. George's
1 CONC  grandson therefore inherited the throne, on George's death in 1760. _______________________
1 CONT
1 CONT George II was born November 10, 1683, the only son of George I and Sophia. His youth was spent in the Hanoverian court in Germany, and he married Caroline of Anspach in 1705. He was truly devoted to Caroline; she bore him three so
1 CONC ns and five daughters, and actively participated in government affairs, before she died in 1737. Like his father, George was very much a German prince, but at the age of 30 when George I ascended the throne, he was young enough to
1 CONC  absorb the English culture that escaped his father. George II died of a stroke on October 25, 1760.
1 CONT
1 CONT George possessed three passions: the army, music and his wife. He was exceptionally brave and has the distinction of being the last British sovereign to command troops in the field (at Dettingen against the French in 1743). He inh
1 CONC erited his father's love of opera, particularly the work of George Frederick Handel, who had been George I's court musician in Hanover. Caroline proved to be his greatest asset. She revived traditional court life (which had all bu
1 CONC t vanished under George I, was fiercely intelligent and an ardent supporter of Robert Walpole. Walpole continued in the role of Prime Minister at Caroline's behest, as George was loathe keeping his father's head Cabinet member. Th
1 CONC e hatred George felt towards his father was reciprocated by his son, Frederick, Prince of Wales, who died in 1751.
1 CONT
1 CONT Walpole retired in 1742, after establishing the foundation of the modern constitutional monarchy: a Cabinet responsible to a Parliament, which was, in turn, responsible to an electorate. At that time, the system was far from truly
1 CONC  democratic; the electorate was essentially the voice of wealthy landowners and mercantilists. The Whig party was firmly in control, although legitimist Tories attempted one last Jacobite rebellion in 1745, by again trying to rest
1 CONC ore a Stuart to the throne. Prince Charles Edward Stuart, known as the Young Pretender or Bonnie Prince Charlie, landed in Scotland and marched as far south as Derby, causing yet another wave of Anti-Catholicism to wash over Engla
1 CONC nd. The Scots retreated, and in 1746, were butchered by the Royal Army at Culloden Moor. Bonnie Prince Charlie escaped to France and died in Rome. The Tories became suspect due to their associations with Jacobitism, ensuring oliga
1 CONC rchic Whig rule for the following fifty years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Walpole managed to keep George out of continental conflicts for the first twelve years of the reign, but George declared war on Spain in 1739, against Walpole's wishes. The Spanish war extended into the 1740's as a component of th
1 CONC e War of Austrian Succession, in which England fought against French dominance in Europe. George shrank away from the situation quickly: he negotiated a hasty peace with France, to protect Hanover. The 1750's found England again a
1 CONC t war with France, this time over imperial claims. Fighting was intense in Europe, but North America and India were also theatres of the war. Government faltering in response to the French crisis brought William Pitt the Elder, la
1 CONC ter Earl of Chatham, to the forefront of British politics.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thackeray describes George II and Walpole as such, in The Four Georges "... how he was a choleric little sovereign; how he shook his fist in the face of his father's courtiers; how he kicked his coat and wig about in his rages; an
1 CONC d called everybody thief, liar, rascal with whom he differed: you will read in all the history books; and how he speedily and shrewdly reconciled himself with the bold minister, whom he had hated during his father's life, and by w
1 CONC hom he was served during fifteen years of his own with admirable prudence, fidelity, and success. But for Robert Walpole, we should have had the Pretender back again."
1 CONT
1 CONT George II of Great Britain From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT George II, George Augustus (10 November 1683 - 25 October 1760; reigned 11 June  1727 - 25 October 1760), was the second Hanoverian king of the Kingdom of Great Britain and Kingdom of Ireland. He was concurrently Duke and Elector 
1 CONC of Hanover and Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg in Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince Georg August (George Augustus) was born at Schloss Herrenhausen, Hanover, the son of Georg Ludwig, then-Hereditary Prince of Hanover and Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, and his wife Princess Sophia Dorothea of Brunswick-Zell. H
1 CONC e became Hereditary Prince of Hanover when his father succeeded as Duke of Hanover and Elector, 23 January 1687. Under the provisions of the Act of Settlement, Prince George Augustus of Hanover was naturalized a British subject in
1 CONC  1705 and became a Knight of the Garter on 4 April 1706. Queen Anne/Anne_I_of_Great_Britain created him Duke and Marquess of Cambridge, Earl of Milford Haven, Vicount Northallerton, and Baron of Tewkesbury in the peerage of Englan
1 CONC d on 9 November 1706. When his father ascended the British throne as King George I, he automatically became Duke of Cornwall and Duke of Rothesay, as the eldest son of the British Sovereign. His father created him Prince of Wales 
1 CONC and Earl of Chester on 27 September 1714 at the Palace of Westminster. The new Prince of Wales was by this time a man of thirty, and had been married for several years to Princess Caroline of Brandenberg-Ansbach with whom he had t
1 CONC hree sons and five daughters.:
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick, Prince of Wales (1 February 1 1707 - 31 March 1751); married 1736, Princess Augusta of Saxe-Gotha-Altenberg (30 November 1719 - 8 February 1772) Anne, Princess Royal Princess_of_Orange (2 November 1709 - 12 January 1759
1 CONC )married 1734, Prince Willem IV of Orange-Nassau (11 September 1711-12 October  1751) Princess Amelia Sophia Eleanor (10 July 1711-31 October 1746). Princess Caroline Elizabeth (21 June 1713-28 December 1757). Prince George Willia
1 CONC m (13 November 1717 - 17 February 1718). Prince William Augustus, Duke of Cumberland (26 April 1721 - 31 October 1765). Princess Mary (5 March 1723-14 January 1772); married 1740, Frederick II, Landgrave of Hesse-Cassel (14 August
1 CONC  1720-31 October 1785); and had issue Princess Louisa (18 December 1724-19 December 1751); married 1743 King Frederick V of Denmark and Norway (31 March 1723-14 January 1766)
1 CONT
1 CONT George II had a poor relationship with his father, and his own eldest son, Frederick, Prince of Wales, would have an equally bad relationship with him. After the death of George I, it was discovered that the late king had planned 
1 CONC to send his son into exile in the colonies.
1 CONT
1 CONT George was neither cultured nor intelligent, but his wife, Caroline, was both. She exercised political influence by her friendship with the prime minister, Robert Walpole, even after a quarrel with the king which resulted in the P
1 CONC rince and Princess of Wales - as they then were - being thrown out of their royal apartments. She was also Regent whenever George was on one of his frequent visits abroad in Hanover. They held a rival court at their home in Leices
1 CONC ter House.
1 CONT
1 CONT George II succeeded to the throne on his father's death in 1727, but a battle of wills continued with his son and heir, Frederick, Prince of Wales, ending only with Frederick's untimely death in 1751. Frederick's eldest son, the f
1 CONC uture George III _of_the_United_Kingdom, was thereupon created Prince of Wales and Earl of Chester. However, young George's mother, the Dowager Princess of Wales, mistrusted her father-in-law and did her best to keep them apart.
1 CONT
1 CONT George II is remembered as the last British Sovereign to lead his own troops onto the battlefield, which he did at the Battle of Dettingen. He was accompanied in this exploit by his military-minded younger son, William Augustus, D
1 CONC uke of Cumberland.
1 CONT
1 CONT The most important event of George's reign, however, was the Jacobite Rebellion of 1745, which almost resulted in his overthrow by the Stuart claimant to the throne and culminated in the Battle of Culloden (1746), the last battle 
1 CONC to be fought on British soil. The Duke of Cumberland, entrusted with command on his father's behalf, defeated the army of Bonnie Prince Charlie, effectively putting an end to the Jacobite resistance.
1 CONT
1 CONT George II, despite being happily married, always had an interest in other women, and kept many mistresses. On the premature death of his wife, Queen Caroline, in 1737, he was distraught. When she reputedly told him, on her deathbe
1 CONC d, that he should re-marry, he famously replied, "Non, j'aurai des maitresses" ("No, I will have mistresses")! He died on 25 October 1760 from a stroke suffered while sitting in his toilet and was buried at Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was succeeded by his grandson, George III/George_III_of_the_United_Kingdom.
0 @N6000000006127657531@ NOTE Georg II, eng. George II, George Augustus, född 10 november 1683 på Schloss Herrenhausen i Hannover, död 25 oktober 1760 på Kensington Palace, London, var kung av Storbritannien och Irland från 1727, kurfurste av Hannover från 11 
1 CONC juni 1727 tillsin död. Han kröntes 4 oktober 1727.Han var den andre brittiske monarken av huset Hannover och son till Georg I av Storbritannien. Hans seger i slaget vid Dettingen 27 juni 1743 under det österrikiska tronföljdskrige
1 CONC t var det sista slag som leddes av en brittisk kung. Han vardessutom den siste brittiske monarken som var född utanför Storbritannien.Georg II utövade lite makt över politiken under början av sin regenttid. Regeringen styrdes i st
1 CONC ället av Storbritanniens förste (inofficielle) "premiärminister", Sir Robert Walpole.Georg II var känd för sina många konflikter med sin far och senare med sin son. Han hade ett bättre förhållande till sin hustru, Caroline av Ansb
1 CONC ach, som han gifte sig med 1705 och var mycket fäst vid, trots att han var notoriskt otrogen.
