1 CONT Duke of Cumberland
0 @N6000000005598973770@ NOTE he was also Marquess of Berkhampstead, Earl of Kennington, Viscount Trematon and Baron Alderney, Field Marshal, and C-in-C cmd'd the Army at Fontenoy and Culledon
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince William Augustus, Duke of Cumberland From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Prince William Augustus, Duke of Cumberland (April 15, 1721- October 31, 1765), a younger son of King George II of Great Britain and Queen Caroline, was a noted military leader.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born in London, England, where his parents had moved after his grandfather, King George I was invited to take the British throne. He became Duke of Cumberland when five years old, was well educated, and became his parents' 
1 CONC favourite (so much so that his father would later consider ways of making him his heir in preference to his eldest brother). From childhood, he showed physical courage and ability. He was intended by the king and queen for the off
1 CONC ice of Lord High Admiral, and in 1740 he sailed as a volunteer in the fleet under the command of Sir John Norris; but he quickly became dissatisfied with the navy, and early in 1742 he began an army career. In December 1742 he bec
1 CONC ame a major-general, and in the following year he first saw active service in Germany. George II and the "martial boy" shared in the glory of the Battle of Dettingen (June 27, 1743), and Cumberland, who was wounded in the action, 
1 CONC was reported as a hero in England, thus founding his military popularity. After the battle he was made lieutenant-general.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1745, having been made captain-general of the British land forces at home and in the field, the duke was again in Flanders as commander-in-chief of the allied British, Hanoverian, Austrian and Dutch troops. Advancing to the rel
1 CONC ief of Tournay, which was besieged by Marshal Saxe, he engaged that great general in the Battle of Fontenoy on May 11, 1745. Had the duke been supported by the allies in his marvellously courageous attack on the superior positions
1 CONC  of the French army, Fontenoy would probably not have been recorded as a defeat for Britain. Cumberland himself was in the midst of the column which penetrated the French centre, and his conduct of the inevitable retreat was unusu
1 CONC ally cool and skilful. The brigade which he commanded to capture the redoubt included the Scottish Highland Black Watch regiment, which in 1743 had been taken away from its previous role of policing its home area to join the force
1 CONC s on the continent. The courage of the Highlanders in the battle greatly impressed the Duke of Cumberland. Shortly afterwards, in the autumn of 1745, the Black Watch was transferred to the South of England for defence against poss
1 CONC ible French invasion while the British were preoccupied further north.
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite being a strict disciplinarian, the young duke was an inspiration to his men and created a very lively esprit de corps. As a general he had courage and resolution; while he sometimes lacked wisdom and tact, he displayed an 
1 CONC energy and power in military affairs which pointed him out to the British people as the one commander upon whom they could rely to put a decisive stop to the successful career of Prince Charles Edward Stuart (The Young Pretender) 
1 CONC in the Jacobite Rebellion of 1745 - 1746. John (Earl) Ligonier wrote of him at this time, Ou je suis fort trompé ou il se forme là un grand capitaine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Recalled from Flanders, Cumberland immediately proceeded with his preparations for quelling the insurrection. He joined the midland army under Sir John Ligonier, and immediately began pursuit of the enemy. But the retreat of Charl
1 CONC es Edward from Derby disconcerted his plans; and it was not till they had reached Penrith, and the advanced portion of his army had been repulsed on Clifton Moor, that Cumberland became aware how hopeless an attempt to overtake th
1 CONC e retreating Highlanders would then be. Carlisle having been retaken, he retired to London, till the news of the defeat of Hawley at Falkirk roused again the fears of the English people, and centred the hopes of Britain on the roy
1 CONC al duke. He was appointed commander of the forces in Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Having arrived in Edinburgh on January 30, 1746, he at once proceeded in search of the "Young Pretender". He made a detour to Aberdeen, where he spent some time training the well-equipped forces now under his command for the pecul
1 CONC iar nature of the warfare in which they were about to engage. What the old and experienced generals of his time had failed even to understand, the young Duke of Cumberland, still only twenty-four years of age, accomplished easily.
1 CONC  He prepared his army to withstand the aggressive charges on which all Highland successes depended; and he reorganised the forces and restored their discipline and self-confidence in a few weeks.
1 CONT
1 CONT On April 8, 1746, he set out from Aberdeen towards Inverness, and on April 15 he fought the decisive Battle of Culloden, in which the forces of the Pretender were completely destroyed. Cumberland told his troops to take notice tha
1 CONC t the Jacobite enemy's orders were to give no quarter to the "troops of the elector", and they took the hint to reciprocate; there is no evidence of any such orders. On account of the merciless severity with which the fugitives we
1 CONC re treated, Cumberland received the nickname of "Butcher". This taunt was used for political purposes in England, and Cumberland's own brother, Frederick, Prince of Wales (who had been refused permission to take a military role on
1 CONC  his father's behalf), seems to have encouraged the virulent attacks upon the duke. Like Oliver Cromwell in Ireland, Cumberland dared to act in a way which would be held against him for the rest of his life, and terrorised an obst
1 CONC inate and unyielding enemy into submission. How real was the danger of a protracted guerrilla warfare in the Highlands may be judged from the explicit declarations of Jacobite leaders that they intended to continue the struggle. A
1 CONC s it was, the war came to an end almost at once.
1 CONT
1 CONT Cumberland preserved the strictest discipline in his camp. He was inflexible in the execution of what he deemed to be his duty, without favour to any man. At the same time he exercised his influence in favour of clemency in specia
1 CONC l cases that were brought to his notice. Some years later James Wolfe spoke of the duke as "for ever doing noble and generous actions".
1 CONT
1 CONT The Duke's victorious efforts were acknowledged by his being voted an income of £40,000 per annum in addition to his revenue as a prince of the royal house. The duke took no part in the Flanders campaign of 1746, but in 1747 he ag
1 CONC ain opposed the still victorious Marshal Saxe; and received a heavy defeat at the Battle of Lauffeld, or Val, near Maestricht (July 2, 1747).
1 CONT
1 CONT During the ten years of peace from 1748 Cumberland occupied himself chiefly with his duties as captain-general, and the result of his work was clearly shown in the conduct of the army in the Seven Years' War. His unpopularity, whi
1 CONC ch had steadily increased since Culloden, interfered greatly with his success in politics, and when the death of the Prince of Wales brought the latter's son, a minor, next in succession to the throne, the duke was not able to sec
1 CONC ure for himself the contingent regency, which was vested in Augusta of Saxe-Gotha, the Princess Dowager of Wales, who considered him an enemy. In 1757, the Seven Years' War having broken out, Cumberland was placed at the head of a
1 CONC  motley army of allies to defend Hanover. At the Battle of Hastenbeck, near Hamelin, on July 26 1757, he was defeated by the superior forces of D'Estrées. In September of the same year his defeat had almost become disgrace. Driven
1 CONC  from point to point, and at last hemmed in by the French under Richelieu, he capitulated at Klosterzeven on September 8, 1757, agreeing to disband his army and to evacuate Hanover.
1 CONT
1 CONT His disgrace was completed on his return to England by his father George II's refusal to be bound by the terms of the duke's agreement. In chagrin and disappointment he retired into private life, after having formally resigned the
1 CONC  public offices he held. In his retirement he made no attempt to justify his conduct, applying in his own case the discipline he had enforced in others. For a few years he lived quietly at Windsor, and subsequently in London, taki
1 CONC ng but little part in politics. He did much, however, to displace the Bute ministry and that of Grenville, and endeavoured to restore Pitt to office. Public opinion had now set in his favour, and he became almost as popular as he 
1 CONC had been in his youth. Shortly before his death the duke was requested to open negotiations with Pitt for a return to power. This was, however, unsuccessful.
1 CONT
1 CONT Biography A Life of the duke of Cumberland by Andrew Henderson was published in 1766, and anonymous (Richard Rolt) Historical Memoirs appeared in 1767. See especially A. N. Campbell Maclachlan, William Augustus, Duke of Cumberland
1 CONC  (1876).
1 CONT Text originally from 1911encyclopedia
0 @N6000000006127657391@ NOTE Prinzessin Maria von Hannover (* 5. August 1722 in London (Leicester House); † 14. Januar 1772 in Hanau) war Landgräfin von Hessen-Kassel.Sie war eine Tochter von König Georg II. von Großbritannien und dessen Gattin Caroline von A
1 CONC nsbach.Am 28. Juni 1740 heiratete sie in Kassel den Landgrafen Friedrich II. von Hessen-Kassel. Mit ihm hatte sie folgendeKinderWilhelm *1741 Wilhelm *1743 Karl *1744 Friedrich *1747 Als 1754 bekannt wurde, dass der Erbprinz, Land
1 CONC graf Friedrich II. zum katholischen Glauben konvertiert war, trennte seine Frau sich von ihm und verließ ihn mit ihren gemeinsamen Kindern.1736 starb mit Johann Reinhard III. von Hanau der letzte männliche Vertreter des Hanauer Gr
1 CONC afenhauses. Aufgrund des Erbvertrags von 1643 fiel der Hanau-Münzenberger Landesteil an Hessen-Kassel. Der Schwiegervater von Landgräfin Maria, LandgrafWilhelm VIII. von Hessen-Kassel, übertrug, um seinen Sohn für die Konversion a
1 CONC bzustrafen, soviel wie möglich von seinem Erbe direkt auf den Enkel, Wilhelm (IX./I.). Dazu zählte auch die Grafschaft Hanau. Für die Zeit von dessen Minderjährigkeitbestellte Wilhelm VIII. Maria als Vormünderin. Landgräfin Maria 
1 CONC regierte so für ihren Sohn, Wilhelm (IX./I.), vormundschaftlich von 1760 - 1764 die Grafschaft Hanau.Ihre letzten Lebensjahre verbrachte sie im Rumpenheimer Schloss. Sie liegt in der Gruft der Marienkirche in Hanau begraben.
0 @N6000000006127657159@ NOTE Louise föddes den 18 december 1724 på Leicester House i England som dotter till kurfursten av Hannover och blivande kungen i Storbritannien George II av Hannover och Carolina av Brandenburg-Ansbach.En vecka före sin nittonårsdag, 
1 CONC den 11 december 1743, gifte hon sig med den danske kronprinsen Fredrik (V) av Oldenburg, och de fick med tiden följande barn:1) Christian 1745 (död 2 år)2) Sofia Magdalena 1746-1813 (g.m. Gustav III)3) Karolina 1747-1820 (g.m. Wil
1 CONC helm I av Hessen-Kassel) 4) Christian VII 1749-18085) Louise 1750-1831 (g.m. Karl av Hessen-Kassel)Hon vann folkets kärlek genom att hon, i motsats till sin svärmoder, Sofia Magdalena, livligt intresserade sig för allt som var dan
1 CONC skt. Strax efter sin ankomst till Danmark avskedade hon sina engelska betjänter och lärde sig danska och taladesedan alltid detta språk samt visade sig mild och vänlig mot alla. Hon älskade musik och kallade 1747 in ett italienskt
1 CONC  operasällskap till hovteatern. Hon såg också personligen till att hjälpa till med sina barns uppfostran och hon kallade bland annat in en lärare som undervisade dem i det danska språket. Honförsökte in i det längsta att förhindra
1 CONC  att äktenskapet mellan hennes dotter Sofia Magdalena och den svenske kronprinsen Gustav (III) skulle bli av.Hon kunde emellertid inte fängsla sin make och hindra hans utsvävningar, men i motsats till Lovisa av Mecklenburg visade 
1 CONC hon stort överseende med makens otrohet och sökte tröst i omsorg om sina barn och i vidsträckt välgörenhet. Hon dog den 19december 1751, dagen efter sin 27-årsdag. Hennes död väckte allmän sorg. Gravplats: Danmark - Roskilde kated
1 CONC ral  
